{"maxId":287,"entries":{"chapter-50-study-mobility-assessment-and-management|Chapter 50 Study: Mobility Assessment and Management":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Mobility is moving the body through space, from bed movement and transfers to walking and stairs. Its limitations threaten independence and predict adverse outcomes (p741).":["u3"],"copy|Disability depends on the person and environment, not simply an isolated impairment; biomechanical, biomedical and psychosocial perspectives are complementary (p741, p746).":["u4"],"copy|Evaluate cardiopulmonary, musculoskeletal, neurologic and psychological contributors; use gait speed, the short physical performance battery or Timed Up and Go as appropriate (p741).":["u5"],"copy|Triage into nonambulatory, ambulatory or vigorous mobility. Nonambulatory assessment emphasizes bed movement/transfers; more able patients need progressively challenging walking tasks (p751–752).":["u6"],"copy|Usual walking speed should generally be at least 1 m/s; the chapter illustrates a 4-m walk in under 4 seconds (p750).":["u7"],"copy|Gait aid use, speed below 1 m/s, short steps or asymmetry indicate a need for closer assessment (p751).":["u8"],"copy|For apparently normal walkers, higher-level tests include a 30-second single-foot stand, tandem walking or over 450–500 m in 6 minutes (p751).":["u9"],"copy|Performance tests measure capacity under test conditions, not actual daily activity; standardize instructions and consider cooperation and fluctuating performance (p745).":["u10"],"copy|Address impairments while practicing mobility tasks; combine strength, balance, endurance and flexibility training as needed (p755).":["u11"],"copy|Use assistive/orthotic devices, environmental adaptations and caregiver training to improve stability, participation and weight-bearing pain (p741, p755).":["u12"],"copy|Deconditioning is almost always present as a consequence of reduced mobility and inactivity and is treatable in many sick or frail older adults. General conditioning and progressively complex walking practice may help (p755).":["u13"],"matching-the-mobility-test-to-the-clinical-question|Matching the mobility test to the clinical question":["u268"],"copy|The Short Physical Performance Battery combines a timed 4-m walk, 5 chair rises and 3 standing balance challenges. A total below 10 points signals risk of disability. A change of 1 point is substantial. Performance measures can help characterize sarcopenia, whereas Barthel mobility items describe transfers, walking or wheeling, and stairs (Table 50-2, p743).":["u269"],"copy|In the Timed Up and Go, start in a chair, stand, walk 10 feet, turn, return and sit. Table 50-2 gives under 10 seconds as normal, under 20 seconds for community mobility and over 30 seconds for needing assistance. A result between the community and assistance thresholds warrants assessment without automatically assigning dependence (p744).":["u270"],"copy|The six-minute walk measures distance on an uninterrupted course. The table gives at least 300 m for community ambulators and changes of 20 m and 50 m as small and substantial. The long-distance corridor walk instead records distance over 2 minutes followed by time for 400 m. Each extra minute to finish was associated with a 52% higher rate of mobility disability (p744).":["u271"],"copy|Compare like with like when following gait speed. A standing versus rolling start and instructions can change the result. Table 50-2 calls speeds below 0.6 m/s slow and below 0.4 m/s very slow. A change of 0.05 m/s is small but meaningful and 0.10 m/s is substantial (p744).":["u272"],"copy|Watch each foot through stance and swing. Double support means that both feet touch the ground at the same time. It occupies a larger fraction of the cycle with age as speed slows and steps shorten. A patient can take shorter steps without necessarily widening the lateral distance between footprints (p747–748).":["u273"],"from-impairment-to-the-activity-the-patient-has-lost|From impairment to the activity the patient has lost":["u274"],"choosing-a-measure-and-interpreting-disagreement-between-measures|Choosing a measure and interpreting disagreement between measures":["u275"],"fear-confidence-and-actual-exposure-to-walking|Fear, confidence and actual exposure to walking":["u276"],"population-findings-and-prognosis|Population findings and prognosis":["u277"],"what-the-footprints-and-joint-movements-reveal|What the footprints and joint movements reveal":["u278"],"how-modest-impairments-add-up|How modest impairments add up":["u279"],"relating-measured-speed-to-the-patient-39-s-day|Relating measured speed to the patient's day":["u280"],"working-backward-from-an-abnormal-task|Working backward from an abnormal task":["u281"],"choosing-a-treatment-at-the-level-of-the-problem|Choosing a treatment at the level of the problem":["u282"],"exam-traps|Exam traps":["u29"],"copy|A measure that assigns higher scores for confidence can move in the opposite direction from a fear or falls-efficacy scale (Table 50-3, p745).":["u283"],"copy|Distinguish the threshold for increased future risk, the tested person's baseline and a meaningful change after treatment (p743–744, p746).":["u284"],"copy|A shorter step on the same side on every cycle differs from unpredictable step-to-step variation (Figure 50-3, p748).":["u285"],"copy|A six-minute distance for community ambulation and a six-minute distance used to challenge an apparently vigorous walker answer different questions (p744, p751).":["u286"],"copy|Improvement in walking speed after medication may uncover persistent difficulty turning or balancing (p755).":["u287"],"copy|Gait speed is an integrative functional marker, not a diagnosis of the responsible impairment; assessment scales help choose tasks and monitor treatment (p750, p752).":["u14"],"copy|In a pooled community cohort, each 0.1 m/s faster speed was associated with 13% lower mortality risk; this is an association, not a guaranteed treatment effect (p746).":["u15"],"copy|With aging, step length and speed decline and double-support time increases; step length and stride length describe different distances (p747).":["u16"],"exam-cited-book-images|Exam-cited book images":["u17"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, Table 50-2, p.743":["u18","u35"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, Table 50-2, p.744":["u19","u36"],"past-exam-questions|Past exam questions":["u42"],"recall-drill|Recall drill":["u46"],"copy|CHAPTER 50 / HAZZARD":["u24"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 50; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u48"],"copy|Definition & consequences":["u26"],"copy|Assessment":["u27"],"copy|Management":["u28"],"copy|Start with an activity the person wants to do.":["u52"],"copy|“I can no longer get to my usual place of worship” gives a better starting point than “my legs are weak.”":["u53"],"copy|Ask about getting out of a chair, reaching the door, negotiating the steps, boarding transport and walking from the stop.":["u54"],"copy|The limiting point could be painful loading of one knee, breathlessness after a short distance, fear on an uneven pavement or the absence of a handrail.":["u55"],"copy|Each would produce the same loss of participation and a different treatment plan.":["u56"],"copy|Table 50-1 uses the International Classification of Functioning, Disability and Health to separate these observations.":["u57"],"copy|Strength and joint motion describe the body, transfers and walking describe tasks, and returning to a valued role describes participation (p741–742).":["u58"],"copy|Ask how the activity is accomplished as well as whether it is possible.":["u59"],"copy|Someone who still shops but now uses a trolley for support and takes a rest before walking home has already adapted.":["u60"],"copy|A yes/no independence question can conceal that loss of reserve.":["u61"],"copy|Conversely, an inability to shop alone may chiefly reflect inaccessible transport.":["u62"],"copy|To set a goal, establish the walking or transfer requirement, the help available, and what would count as a worthwhile improvement for this particular person.":["u63"],"copy|Maintaining a safe independent toilet transfer can profoundly affect care needs even when community walking is no longer attainable (p742–743, p750–751).":["u64"],"copy|Self-report, observation by a professional and timed performance provide different descriptions of mobility.":["u65"],"copy|A patient can report an activity that seldom occurs in a clinic, including how tiring it is or how much help is needed.":["u66"],"copy|A nurse may observe several transfers over a shift and identify how much lifting assistance is required.":["u67"],"copy|A timed walk can detect a small change that neither person would describe as a change in independence.":["u68"],"copy|Each source can be accurate even when they disagree.":["u69"],"copy|Ask whether the measurement concerns the same task, circumstances and period of time before attributing the difference to unreliable reporting (p742–745).":["u70"],"copy|Table 50-2 illustrates why choosing a test starts with a question.":["u71"],"copy|PROMIS mobility covers a wide range of demands and can use either a short form or computer-adaptive testing.":["u72"],"copy|The Avlund scales ask separately about fatigue and help across six activities.":["u73"],"copy|Someone may need no hands-on help but tire with every task.":["u74"],"copy|Asking about changing the way one walks half a mile or climbs stairs can reveal preclinical limitation.":["u75"],"copy|At the other end of the scale, asking a wheelchair user about walking a mile will repeatedly give the lowest score even if moving between bed and chair has improved considerably (p742–743).":["u76"],"copy|The Barthel mobility items cover walking or wheeling, transfers and stairs.":["u77"],"copy|They contribute to a basic ADL score.":["u78"],"copy|The table gives a total possible score of 100, with higher scores representing more independence, and a minimal clinically important difference of 9.8 points.":["u79"],"copy|FIM also describes the amount of help required in daily function.":["u80"],"copy|In the Minimum Data Set, staff can use individual bed mobility, transfer and locomotion items to describe care needs in a skilled nursing facility.":["u81"],"copy|These tools answer practical questions about assistance.":["u82"],"copy|A good score does not establish adequate endurance for a journey to the shops (p743).":["u83"],"copy|In September 2024 Q28, match the instrument to the question being asked.":["u84"],"copy|The SPPB directly samples lower-extremity performance through walking, chair rises and standing balance.":["u85"],"copy|It can contribute to the assessment of physical performance in sarcopenia.":["u86"],"copy|The Barthel Index assesses basic function, FIM measures independence, and the TUG times a sequence of movements.":["u87"],"copy|A TUG result does not tell you whether a person can dress, bathe or use the toilet independently.":["u88"],"copy|Similarly, a low SPPB can arise from pain, neurologic disease or poor cardiopulmonary reserve.":["u89"],"copy|It cannot establish the cause of the performance deficit by itself (p742–745, p752).":["u90"],"copy|June 2025 Q51 requires keeping time, distance and test together.":["u91"],"copy|The item describes a TUG of 25 seconds.":["u92"],"copy|It falls beyond the book's community-mobility benchmark of under 20 seconds, without crossing the over 30 seconds assistance benchmark.":["u93"],"copy|Watch where the delay occurs: pushing out of the chair, initiating gait and turning place different demands on the patient.":["u94"],"copy|The alternative of 400 m during a six-minute walk exceeds the table's 300-m community benchmark.":["u95"],"copy|A high SPPB score likewise represents better performance.":["u96"],"copy|Finally, the initial timed segment of the long-distance corridor walk records how far the patient travels; its subsequent 400-m segment records how long completion takes.":["u97"],"copy|Substituting one measure for the other changes the interpretation (Table 50-2, p743–744).":["u98"],"copy|Ask whether the patient is afraid of falling, how confident they are in a specific activity and whether they have stopped doing it.":["u99"],"copy|The answers may diverge.":["u100"],"copy|A person who feels reasonably safe indoors may have given up outdoor walking altogether.":["u101"],"copy|Another may continue walking despite considerable fear because no one else can obtain groceries.":["u102"],"copy|The absence of a recent fall in the first person gives little reassurance about performance outdoors; the threatening activity has largely disappeared from daily life (p745, p749–750).":["u103"],"copy|The scoring direction matters in Table 50-3.":["u104"],"copy|On the Falls Efficacy Scale, 1 indicates great confidence and 10 no confidence for each item, and a total above 80 identifies increased fall risk.":["u105"],"copy|On the Activities-Specific Balance Confidence Scale, by contrast, 100 marks complete confidence.":["u106"],"copy|The modified Gait Efficacy Scale also gives higher scores for greater confidence.":["u107"],"copy|A higher number therefore cannot be called “better” without identifying the scale.":["u108"],"copy|A performance observation can then test the activity the person fears, while the interview identifies what is actually being avoided at home (p745).":["u109"],"copy|Distinguish basic indoor mobility from higher-level outdoor mobility.":["u110"],"copy|In the estimates cited, dependence in getting around indoors rose from 5% among adults aged 65–74 to 30% among those aged 85 or older.":["u111"],"copy|Among community-dwelling adults aged 65 or older, approximately 5% needed help with bed or chair transfers and 7.5% needed help getting around indoors.":["u112"],"copy|Among institutionalized people older than 65, about 80% needed help with these activities.":["u113"],"copy|The task and setting are essential parts of the estimate.":["u114"],"copy|A clinic caring for independently living adults will encounter limitations in distance and stairs that an indoor dependence question misses (p745–746).":["u115"],"copy|A person who reports no mobility concern can still have a slow measured walk.":["u116"],"copy|In the studies summarized, performance predicted later ADL difficulty, hospitalization and mortality even among initially nondisabled people.":["u117"],"copy|Poor baseline performance was associated with roughly twice the hospitalization risk over 4 years in one population study.":["u118"],"copy|Slowing may reflect interacting illness, inactivity or declining reserve.":["u119"],"copy|For follow-up, record the method and compare the person's trajectory with their previous performance.":["u120"],"copy|The pooled relationship between speed and survival provides prognostic information; it cannot tell the clinician how much longer this individual will live after walking faster on a test (p745–746).":["u121"],"copy|Figure 50-1 follows weight transfer across the gait cycle.":["u122"],"copy|At initial contact, controlled ankle and knee movement accepts the load.":["u123"],"copy|During stance, the hip abductors steady the pelvis over the supporting limb.":["u124"],"copy|Late stance brings hip extension and ankle push-off; during swing the foot must clear the ground and advance until it can take weight again.":["u125"],"copy|Watch when the abnormality occurs.":["u126"],"copy|Failure to control the foot as it meets the floor produces a slap, whereas inadequate clearance during swing can produce a drag.":["u127"],"copy|Both can involve weak dorsiflexors, but the phase in which the difficulty is seen guides the examination (p747, p753).":["u128"],"copy|Figures 50-2 and 50-3 separate distance from rhythm.":["u129"],"copy|Compare the right-to-left and left-to-right steps for length, then look at several successive steps.":["u130"],"copy|A limp may give a consistently shorter step on the same side.":["u131"],"copy|Unpredictable variation from one step to the next gives a different pattern associated with falls and disability.":["u132"],"copy|Step width describes the lateral space between footprints.":["u133"],"copy|A short step and a wide base are independent findings, and neither alone establishes the cause of the gait disorder (p747–748).":["u134"],"copy|June 2023 Q51 asks which change tends to accompany aging.":["u135"],"copy|The supported finding is a larger fraction of the cycle in double support, with both feet on the ground.":["u136"],"copy|Common accompanying changes are slower speed, shorter steps, less hip extension and more external rotation of the foot.":["u137"],"copy|Older walkers may generate less power at the ankle and compensate with hip flexion.":["u138"],"copy|The item reverses the rotation or assigns the compensatory movement to the wrong joint in its distractors.":["u139"],"copy|More time on two feet can assist stability while making walking less energy-efficient.":["u140"],"copy|The patient may therefore use more of their available energy to cover a familiar distance (p747–748).":["u141"],"copy|Organize the examination around sensory inputs, central processing and effectors.":["u142"],"copy|Vision, vestibular input and distal sensation tell a person where their body is.":["u143"],"copy|The brain integrates those signals with attention, timing and postural responses.":["u144"],"copy|Strength, range of motion, pain and cardiopulmonary endurance then determine whether the intended movement can be carried out.":["u145"],"copy|A marked deficit at one level may explain the difficulty.":["u146"],"copy|Several less apparent deficits may instead emerge only in combination.":["u147"],"copy|Mild neuropathy may be tolerated on a level, well-lit floor and become limiting on uneven ground in the dark (p748–749, p754).":["u148"],"copy|Walking while talking or doing a mental calculation adds an attentional demand.":["u149"],"copy|A curved path, an obstacle or a faster turn challenges balance and timing.":["u150"],"copy|These tasks can reveal loss of reserve in someone whose ordinary straight walk looks adequate.":["u151"],"copy|The chapter describes associations between gait slowing, executive changes and brain imaging findings such as white matter disease, including in people without overt stroke.":["u152"],"copy|Those observations invite assessment of cognition and vascular risk.":["u153"],"copy|They do not turn a slow walk into a specific imaging diagnosis or prove that correcting one risk will restore mobility (p748–749, p752).":["u154"],"copy|Table 50-4 puts pace, distance and activity side by side.":["u155"],"copy|At roughly 0.67 m/s, six minutes corresponds to about 240 m and the listed activity level is self-care.":["u156"],"copy|At about 0.89 m/s, the corresponding distance is 320 m and the example is household activity.":["u157"],"copy|Around 1.1 m/s or 400 m, the table gives carrying groceries or light yard work; around 1.3 m/s or 480 m, climbing several flights.":["u158"],"copy|These are approximations to the demand of activities.":["u159"],"copy|Directly ask about a person's stairs, handrail, loads and opportunity to rest before deciding whether a particular activity is safe (p750).":["u160"],"copy|Table 50-5 illustrates an energy-based classification.":["u161"],"copy|Its most limited level requires help to move from bed or chair; the next uses a wheelchair indoors but transfers independently.":["u162"],"copy|That difference determines whether another person must be available for the transfer.":["u163"],"copy|At a higher level the person may manage a block, sometimes with a cane, while at still higher levels sustained walking becomes feasible.":["u164"],"copy|At the vigorous end the example is at least 30 minutes at a vigorous pace above 4 METs.":["u165"],"copy|Distance, pace and duration together describe reserve more usefully than a single unqualified statement that a patient “walks” (p750).":["u166"],"copy|Figure 50-4 sorts the first visit by demonstrated ability and the potential for improvement.":["u167"],"copy|For someone who arrives in a wheelchair, first establish what happens when they attempt to rise with appropriate assistance.":["u168"],"copy|For an ambulatory patient, inspect speed, symmetry, step length and the need for a device.":["u169"],"copy|For an apparently vigorous patient, ask about more demanding activities and test the limitation that prompted the visit.":["u170"],"copy|Decide with the person whether the aim is restoration, compensation or both.":["u171"],"copy|The onset and course, a reversible impairment, the ability to take part in practice and the patient's preferences all affect that choice (p751–752).":["u172"],"copy|Table 50-6 offers a primary-care starting point.":["u173"],"copy|With exertional dyspnea or fatigue, consider oxygenation during activity, airflow, anemia and cardiac function.":["u174"],"copy|With hesitancy or unsteadiness, examine tone, rapid tapping, executive function and peripheral sensation.":["u175"],"copy|With stiffness or weight-bearing pain, examine the hip, knee and back, then watch a chair rise and test strength.":["u176"],"copy|Exertional leg symptoms merit attention to pulses and to the possibility of spinal stenosis.":["u177"],"copy|A multidisciplinary assessment can be particularly helpful when several explanations coexist or the patient fails to improve after an apparently relevant impairment is treated (p751–754).":["u178"],"copy|Table 50-7 links a movement to plausible causes rather than forcing a named diagnosis.":["u179"],"copy|High stepping may compensate for foot drop.":["u180"],"copy|A pelvic drop suggests weakness of the abductors on the opposite side of the drop.":["u181"],"copy|A patient can hike a hip to clear a limb with insufficient hip or knee flexion.":["u182"],"copy|Hesitant initiation occurs in several central disorders; a weaving path raises a vestibular question, while a broad base can occur with cerebellar or peripheral sensory disease.":["u183"],"copy|Observe the patient from more than one direction and examine the relevant joints and muscle groups before choosing an orthosis or prescribing task practice (p753).":["u184"],"copy|Table 50-8 gives examples of how an examination changes action.":["u185"],"copy|Check visual fields, depth and lighting when a patient misjudges a step, vibration and touch when foot placement is unreliable, and orthostatic symptoms and blood pressure when the patient feels faint on standing.":["u186"],"copy|Review medicines that could worsen postural hypotension, attention or dark adaptation.":["u187"],"copy|A medication trial for Parkinson-related slowing may improve speed while balance remains impaired; faster movement can expose the patient to injury unless rehabilitation accompanies it.":["u188"],"copy|The chapter gives treatment categories for these mechanisms and refers organ-specific prescribing to the relevant disease assessment (p754–755).":["u189"],"copy|A light touch on a stable surface can improve balance through sensory information even when it supports little weight.":["u190"],"copy|A cane can therefore help a person with sensory loss, while a different patient may need a device primarily to unload a painful joint.":["u191"],"copy|Consider range-of-motion work for contracture, strengthening for loss of force and repeated practice of the actual transfer or turn.":["u192"],"copy|Split-belt treadmill work after stroke is one way of practicing more symmetrical stepping.":["u193"],"copy|For body-weight-supported treadmill approaches the chapter notes that well-delivered physical therapy can offer similar benefit.":["u194"],"copy|Select the treatment for the limiting task and reassess its effect on that task (p754–755).":["u195"],"copy|Table 50-9 moves the assessment into the person's usual environment.":["u196"],"copy|Ask about depression, fear, support, transport, cost and the physical barriers at home.":["u197"],"copy|A ramp or handrail may permit participation immediately, while walking and strengthening are being developed.":["u198"],"copy|An unnecessary lift chair or a helper who performs every transfer can instead remove the very activity needed to retain strength.":["u199"],"copy|For the person unable to walk, practice rolling, scooting, sitting or standing when feasible.":["u200"],"copy|Train the helper in safe transfers, consider appropriate seating or transfer equipment, and protect skin exposed to pressure.":["u201"],"copy|When movement would add suffering in a person who is actively dying, the chapter makes comfort the relevant goal (p750, p753, p755–756).":["u202"],"copy|Book images cited by past exams.":["u30"],"copy|Tap an image to zoom.":["u31"],"copy|1.":["u206"],"copy|What question can reveal preclinical mobility limitation in an independent patient?":["u207"],"copy|2.":["u208"],"copy|Which tasks make up the SPPB?":["u209"],"copy|3.":["u210"],"copy|How do you interpret an SPPB score below ten?":["u211"],"copy|4.":["u212"],"copy|What does the Timed Up and Go include besides walking?":["u213"],"copy|5.":["u214"],"copy|What does a TUG between twenty and thirty seconds suggest?":["u215"],"copy|6.":["u216"],"copy|Which distance changes are meaningful on the six-minute walk?":["u217"],"copy|7.":["u218"],"copy|How is the long-distance corridor walk scored?":["u219"],"copy|8.":["u220"],"copy|Why record walking instructions and starting method?":["u221"],"copy|9.":["u222"],"copy|How does a stride differ from a step?":["u223"],"copy|10.":["u224"],"copy|How does double support change with age?":["u225"],"copy|11.":["u226"],"copy|Why can an adequate clinic walk coexist with restricted activity at home?":["u227"],"copy|12.":["u228"],"copy|What should be observed first when the patient arrives in a wheelchair?":["u229"],"copy|13.":["u230"],"copy|What can reveal difficulty when usual walking appears normal?":["u231"],"copy|14.":["u232"],"copy|Why can too much help worsen mobility?":["u233"],"copy|15.":["u234"],"copy|What should accompany treatment of an identified gait impairment?":["u235"],"copy|Answer":["u238","u240","u242","u244","u246","u248","u250","u252","u254","u256","u258","u260","u262","u264","u266"],"copy|Ask whether the patient has changed how they walk longer distances or climb stairs, even if they still complete those activities. (p743)":["u239"],"copy|Timed four-meter walk, five chair rises and three standing balance challenges. (p743)":["u241"],"copy|It identifies risk of disability. A one-point change is considered substantial in the table. (p743)":["u243"],"copy|Rising from the chair, turning after walking ten feet, returning to the chair and sitting down. (p744)":["u245"],"copy|The time is slower than the book's community-mobility benchmark but does not reach its greater-than-thirty-second assistance benchmark. Assess the tasks that were difficult. (p744)":["u247"],"copy|The table describes 20 m as a small meaningful change and 50 m as substantial. (p744)":["u249"],"copy|Record distance in the first two minutes and then the time to complete 400 m. Slower completion is associated with later disability. (p744)":["u251"],"copy|Standing and rolling starts and different instructions can affect measured speed. Repeat the same protocol when following change. (p744–745)":["u253"],"copy|A step runs between consecutive opposite-foot contacts. A stride spans successive contacts of the same foot. (p747–748)":["u255"],"copy|A greater proportion of the gait cycle is spent with both feet on the ground. Gait speed and step length tend to decrease. (p747)":["u257"],"copy|The test measures performance under defined conditions. The home environment, available support and daily behavior influence actual mobility. (p745, p749–750)":["u259"],"copy|Assess bed and chair movement and whether standing or walking is possible with assistance. Select subsequent tasks accordingly. (p751–752)":["u261"],"copy|Single-leg stance, tandem walking, longer distances or complex tasks such as obstacles, turns and walking while doing another task. (p751–752)":["u263"],"copy|Assistance that replaces a task the patient can perform reduces practice. Repeated disuse can contribute to weakness and loss of function. (p750)":["u265"],"copy|Practice the relevant task and address strength, balance, endurance and flexibility. Adapt the environment or walking aid to the patient's goals and limitations. (p755)":["u267"],"copy|June 2025 Q51 — Printed pages 743, 744.":["u43"],"copy|September 2024 Q28 — Printed pages 743, 744.":["u44"],"copy|June 2023 Q51 — Printed page 747.":["u45"],"copy|September 2024 Q28 · June 2025 Q51":["u32"]}}
